    <h1>Gestational Age Explained</h1>

    <p>Gestational age is the most common way to measure pregnancy. It is counted from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP), not from the date of conception. This guide explains why, how it is calculated, and what it means for your due date.</p>

    <h2>What Is Gestational Age?</h2>

    <p>Gestational age is the age of the pregnancy counted from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP). It is expressed in weeks and days. For example, "22 weeks 3 days" is written as 22+3.</p>

    <p>This is the standard method used by healthcare providers and recommended by ACOG. It is used to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Calculate the estimated due date</li>
      <li>Determine the trimester</li>
      <li>Schedule screening tests and ultrasounds</li>
      <li>Assess fetal growth and development</li>
      <li>Classify term (preterm, early term, full term, etc.)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A pregnancy that runs its usual length lasts from 37 weeks to 42 weeks, counted from the first day of your last period. That is why the due date is a single day on the calendar but is treated as an estimate.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Due date calculator, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/finding-out/due-date-calculator/; NHS, Antenatal care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

    <h2>Why Count from the Last Period?</h2>

    <p>Pregnancy is counted from the LMP because most people know the date of their last period, but very few know the exact date of conception.</p>

    <p>The NHS due date calculator works from the first day of your last period. If you do not know it or are unsure, the NHS advises speaking to a midwife or GP. That date is carried forward to give the estimated due date (EDD).</p>

    <p>The convention creates a fixed two-week offset. The counting starts before conception can happen:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>At the moment of conception, you are already considered <strong>2 weeks pregnant</strong>.</li>
      <li>At the time of a missed period, you are considered <strong>4 weeks pregnant</strong>.</li>
      <li>Your due date is <strong>40 weeks</strong> from your LMP, which is about <strong>38 weeks</strong> from conception.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The offset exists because the calendar starts on a date you can usually name. It does not mean the pregnancy is two weeks older than the baby.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Due date calculator, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/finding-out/due-date-calculator/; NHS, Ultrasound scans in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/ultrasound-scans/</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />

    <h2>How Gestational Age Is Calculated</h2>

    <p><strong>Gestational age</strong> = Reference date − (LMP + cycle adjustment)</p>

    <p>The cycle adjustment accounts for cycles that are not exactly 28 days:</p>

    <p><strong>Cycle-corrected LMP</strong> = LMP + (cycle length − 28)</p>

    <p>For example, if your LMP was January 1 and your cycle is 32 days:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Cycle adjustment = 32 − 28 = 4 days</li>
      <li>Cycle-corrected LMP = January 1 + 4 days = January 5</li>
      <li>Estimated due date = January 5 + 280 days = October 12</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Once the date is set, it is normally left alone. ACOG states that the gestational age and the estimated due date should be determined and documented as soon as the LMP date, the first accurate ultrasound, or both are available, and that later changes should be reserved for rare circumstances.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG, Methods for Estimating the Due Date (Committee Opinion 700), https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>Gestational Age vs. Fetal Age</h2>

    <p>Gestational age and fetal age are different:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Measurement</th>
          <th>Counted from</th>
          <th>At conception</th>
          <th>At missed period</th>
          <th>At due date</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Gestational age</td>
          <td>LMP</td>
          <td>2 weeks</td>
          <td>4 weeks</td>
          <td>40 weeks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Fetal age</td>
          <td>Conception</td>
          <td>0 weeks</td>
          <td>2 weeks</td>
          <td>38 weeks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Healthcare providers use gestational age. When someone says "I am 20 weeks pregnant," they mean gestational age.</p>

    <p>The two numbers differ by about two weeks because the calendar starts at the LMP and conception happens later. Fetal age is counted from conception, so it is the smaller number. A baby described as 20 weeks gestational age is about 18 weeks by fetal age. The clinical record, the scan report and the screening timetable all use gestational age, so the difference matters whenever two dates are compared.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Ultrasound scans in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/ultrasound-scans/</em></p>

    <h2>How Accurate Is Gestational Age?</h2>

    <p>LMP-based dating is reasonably accurate if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your cycles are regular</li>
      <li>You know the date of your last period</li>
      <li>You are not currently breastfeeding or have recently stopped hormonal contraception</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A first-trimester ultrasound is more accurate than LMP-based dating. ACOG recommends changing the due date to the ultrasound date if the two differ by more than 7 days before 14 0/7 weeks, or by more than 5 days before 9 0/7 weeks.</p>

    <h2>Ultrasound Dating Accuracy by Trimester</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Trimester</th>
          <th>Accuracy</th>
          <th>Method</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>First</td>
          <td>± 5-7 days</td>
          <td>Crown-rump length (CRL)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Second</td>
          <td>± 7-10 days</td>
          <td>Biparietal diameter, femur length</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Third</td>
          <td>± 21-30 days</td>
          <td>Least reliable; redate only if over 21 days</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG, Methods for Estimating the Due Date (Committee Opinion 700), https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>Ultrasound Re-dating and the ACOG Rule</h2>

    <p>The NHS offers an ultrasound scan at 11 to 14 weeks to check when your baby is due, to check development, and to see whether you are having more than one baby. It is sometimes called the dating scan. At this scan the sonographer estimates the due date from the baby's measurements, and your due date, originally calculated from the first day of your last period, may be adjusted according to those measurements.</p>

    <p>ACOG and its partner societies give three rules that follow from this:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ultrasound measurement of the embryo or fetus in the first trimester, up to and including 13 6/7 weeks of gestation, is the most accurate method to establish or confirm gestational age.</li>
      <li>Once the gestational age and due date are set, later changes should be reserved for rare circumstances and documented clearly in the medical record.</li>
      <li>A pregnancy without an ultrasound examination that confirms or revises the due date before 22 0/7 weeks of gestational age should be considered suboptimally dated.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>This is why the dating scan is offered early. A scan taken later in pregnancy is less precise, so a late scan is not used to move an established due date.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG, Methods for Estimating the Due Date (Committee Opinion 700), https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date; NHS, Antenatal care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/; NHS, Ultrasound scans in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/ultrasound-scans/</em></p>

    <p>If your scan date and your period date do not match, ask your midwife or doctor which one is being used and why.</p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />

    <h2>IVF and Known-Conception Dating</h2>

    <p>When the conception date is known from a procedure, the LMP calculation is replaced. ACOG states that if a pregnancy resulted from assisted reproductive technology (ART), the ART-derived gestational age should be used to assign the estimated due date. For a pregnancy from in vitro fertilization, the due date is assigned using the age of the embryo and the date of transfer.</p>

    <p>This is the one common situation in which dating does not start from a period. Tell your clinician which transfer date applies to you.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG, Methods for Estimating the Due Date (Committee Opinion 700), https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>How Gestational Age Drives Screening and Care</h2>

    <p>Most tests in pregnancy have a window set by gestational age:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Week of pregnancy</th>
          <th>Test</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Usually before 10 weeks</td>
          <td>Screening for sickle cell and thalassaemia</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Usually between 8 and 12 weeks</td>
          <td>Screening for hepatitis B, HIV and syphilis</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Usually between 11 and 14 weeks</td>
          <td>12-week scan, plus the combined test for Down's syndrome, Edwards' syndrome and Patau's syndrome</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Usually between 14 and 20 weeks</td>
          <td>Quadruple blood screening test, if the combined test was not possible</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Usually between 18 and 21 weeks</td>
          <td>20-week scan</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The 20-week scan is always offered and checks for signs of 11 different conditions, as well as growth. You will be offered at least 10 antenatal appointments if this is your first baby and at least 7 if you have given birth before, plus 2 ultrasound scans.</p>

    <p>Gestational age also sets the boundaries of term. The NHS describes a normal pregnancy as lasting from 37 weeks to 42 weeks from the first day of your last period. A delivery before 37 weeks falls outside that range, and a 41 week appointment is offered if you are still pregnant.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Screening tests in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/screening-tests/; NHS, 20-week scan, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/20-week-scan/; NHS, Antenatal care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/; NHS, Due date calculator, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/finding-out/due-date-calculator/</em></p>

    <h2>When to Talk to Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You are unsure of your LMP date</li>
      <li>Your cycles are irregular</li>
      <li>You have questions about your gestational age or due date</li>
      <li>Your ultrasound dating differs from your LMP dating</li>
      <li>You conceived through IVF and are unsure which dating method has been used</li>
    </ul>

